Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 02:05:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 20/22] pdf: Keep the page maps when a stream dictionary
 changes.

Every change of an object linked into the document drops the page maps,
unless the change is marked as non-structural (only the outline code
does that). pdf_update_stream() changes the Length and Filter of the
stream it updates, so every in-memory stream update drops the maps, and
the next page lookup walks the whole page tree again and loads every
page dictionary: on a 30 000 page document a page load takes 57 ms
instead of 3 ms and the loaded page dictionaries stay in memory. A
stream dictionary is never a node of the page tree, so changes inside it
(Length, Filter, DecodeParms, the resources of a form) now keep the
maps.

Modified for ReePDF on 2026-11-15.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 source/pdf/pdf-object.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/source/pdf/pdf-object.c b/source/pdf/pdf-object.c
index db58b64..f2fd965 100644
--- a/source/pdf/pdf-object.c
+++ b/source/pdf/pdf-object.c
@@ -1839,6 +1839,10 @@ static void prepare_object_for_alteration(fz_context *ctx, pdf_obj *obj, pdf_obj
 			/* This object isn't linked into the document - can't change the
 			 * pagemap. */
 		}
+		else if (pdf_obj_num_is_stream(ctx, doc, parent))
+		{
+			/* The dictionary of a stream is never part of the page tree. */
+		}
 		else if (doc->local_xref && doc->local_xref_nesting > 0)
 		{
 			/* We have a local_xref and it's in force. By convention, we
